  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "present simple: affirmative (common verbs)",
      "title_form": "Presente simple: afirmativo con verbos comunes",
      "title_target": "Present Simple: Affirmative with Common Verbs",
      "description_md_form": "El **presente simple** se usa para hablar de **rutinas** y **acciones habituales**. Para la mayoría de los verbos, la forma es muy simple: usamos el verbo en su forma base con *I, you, we, they*, y añadimos una **-s** al final con *he, she, it*.\n\nEjemplos:\n- *I **make** coffee.*\n- *She **makes** coffee.*\n\nCon *he, she, it*, la regla es: **verbo + -s**. Es la única diferencia. No necesitas palabras como \"do\" o \"does\" para afirmar; solo usas el verbo directamente.",
      "description_md_target": "The **present simple** is used to talk about **routines** and **habitual actions**. For most verbs, the form is very simple: we use the base form with *I, you, we, they*, and we add an **-s** at the end with *he, she, it*.\n\nExamples:\n- *I **make** coffee.*\n- *She **makes** coffee.*\n\nWith *he, she, it*, the rule is: **verb + -s**. That is the only difference. You do not need words like \"do\" or \"does\" for affirmative sentences; you just use the verb directly.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + verb (+ -s for he/she/it)",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "present_simple_affirmative",
